      "id": "b053e57fd10b",
      "name": "hourly-hermes-chat-sync",
      "prompt": "Resolve the current local date/time with `date`. Then synchronize recent Hermes chat activity into the Obsidian vault at /var/home/rainbow/Documents/SecondBrain.\n\nTasks:\n1. Use session_search to browse recent sessions and read the most relevant sessions from the last 24 hours (up to 5 sessions).\n1a. If present, also inspect `~/.hermes/logs/hermes-memory-capture.jsonl`, `~/.hermes/logs/hermes-obsidian-file-events.jsonl`, and `~/.hermes/state/obsidian-dirty-paths.json` for high-signal local hints about recent durable changes.\n2. Capture only durable/high-signal items: workflow changes, configuration facts, verified results, note syncs, cron changes, maintenance actions, and stable follow-ups.\n3. Rewrite `/var/home/rainbow/Documents/SecondBrain/04 Resources/Hermes Chat Live Sync.md` as a curated note with:\n   - frontmatter\n   - last synced timestamp\n   - scope\n   - recent sessions\n   - key durable themes\n   - follow-ups\n   - privacy notes\n4. Ensure today's daily note exists at `/var/home/rainbow/Documents/SecondBrain/01 Daily/<YYYY-MM-DD Day>.md` and contains or updates a `## Hermes Chat Sync` section linking to `[[Hermes Chat Live Sync]]` with 3-5 concise bullets.\n5. Keep the daily note thin: one link bullet plus only the most durable 2-4 items. Do not copy detailed PR/commit/test minutiae into the daily note unless they changed an ongoing operating rule.\n6. Keep the output curated and concise. Do NOT dump raw transcripts or secrets.\n7. Prefer rewriting the live-sync note cleanly rather than appending duplicate sections forever.\n8. Treat `~/.hermes/memories/MEMORY.md` as the canonical agent-facing durable memory store; vault `MEMORY.md` and live-sync notes are curated human-readable surfaces, while session/log/state files are retrieval or telemetry sources.\n\nVerification:\n- Read back the files you wrote/updated and confirm the sync landed.\n- If there are no meaningful new sessions, keep the note stable and only refresh the timestamp if appropriate.",
